🟡 INTERMEDIATE

Q: What is interactive rebase and why use it?

git rebase -i HEAD~3

Lets you edit, reorder, squash, or drop the last 3 commits before pushing. Example: you made 5 messy "wip", "fix typo", "actually fix it" commits — squash them into one clean commit before opening a PR, so reviewers see one logical change.

Common actions in interactive rebase:

pick – keep commit as-is
squash/s – merge into previous commit
reword/r – edit commit message
drop/d – remove commit entirely

Q: What is git blame?

git blame filename.js

Shows who last modified each line and in which commit. Example: a bug appears in checkout.js line 42 — blame tells you which commit (and teammate) introduced it, so you can ask context or check the related PR.

Q: What are Git hooks?
Scripts that run automatically at certain Git events, stored in .git/hooks/. Example: a pre-commit hook that runs a linter and blocks the commit if code style fails — catches issues before they even reach a PR.

Q: How do you create a Git alias?

git config --global alias.co checkout
git config --global alias.st status

Example: git co main instead of git checkout main — small time-saver used constantly across a team.

Q: What is git rm vs deleting a file manually?

git rm filename.js

Deletes the file AND stages the deletion in one step. Deleting manually via file explorer still requires git add afterward to stage the removal.

Q: How do you rename/move a file with Git tracking it?

git mv old-name.js new-name.js

Example: renaming utils.js to helpers.js — this keeps the rename tracked cleanly in history instead of showing as a delete + new file.

Q: What's git fetch --prune?

git fetch --prune

Removes local references to remote branches that have been deleted on the server. Example: after 10 feature branches get merged and deleted on GitHub, your local git branch -r still shows them until you prune.

Q: What is a detached HEAD state?
When you check out a specific commit (not a branch), HEAD points directly to that commit instead of a branch. Example: git checkout a1b2c3d to inspect old code — any new commits made here aren't attached to a branch and can get lost unless you create a branch from that point (git checkout -b hotfix-old).

🔴 ADVANCED

Q: What is git rebase --onto?
Used to move a range of commits to a new base, skipping unwanted ones. Example: your feature branch was accidentally based on another unfinished feature branch instead of main — git rebase --onto main old-feature my-feature replays only your commits directly onto main.

Q: What is git worktree?

git worktree add ../hotfix-dir hotfix-branch

Lets you check out multiple branches into separate folders simultaneously from one repo. Example: you're mid-way through a big feature and need to urgently fix a production bug — instead of stashing everything, you open a second working folder for the hotfix branch and keep both running side by side.

Q: What are Git submodules?

git submodule add https://github.com/org/shared-lib.git libs/shared

Lets you include another Git repo as a subdirectory, tracked at a specific commit. Example: multiple internal projects share a common UI component library — instead of copy-pasting it, each project references it as a submodule pinned to a tested version.

Q: What is git subtree and how is it different from submodules?
Subtree merges another repo's content directly into your repo's history (no separate .git reference needed), unlike submodules which just point to a commit. Generally simpler for consumers, more complex to set up.

Q: What are Git objects internally (blob, tree, commit)?

Blob – raw file content
Tree – a folder structure, listing blobs/trees
Commit – a snapshot pointing to a tree, plus metadata (author, message, parent commit)

Example: understanding this explains why renaming a file with identical content doesn't create a new blob — Git deduplicates by content hash (SHA-1).

Q: What is git gc and git prune?

git gc
git prune

gc (garbage collect) compresses and optimizes the repo's object database; prune removes unreachable objects (e.g., from old resets). Example: a repo with years of history and lots of old branches starts feeling slow — git gc cleans it up.

Q: How do you sign commits with GPG?

git commit -S -m "message"
git config --global commit.gpgsign true

Example: in security-sensitive orgs, signed commits prove the commit really came from that developer's verified key, shown as "Verified" on GitHub.

Q: What is git format-patch / applying patches?

git format-patch -1 <commit-hash>
git apply patch-file.patch

Exports a commit as a .patch file that can be emailed or applied elsewhere. Example: contributing to an open-source project without push access — generate a patch and share it instead of a PR.

Q: What is sparse-checkout?

git sparse-checkout init --cone
git sparse-checkout set folder1 folder2

Lets you check out only specific folders of a massive monorepo instead of the whole thing. Example: a company monorepo has 40 services — a frontend dev only needs apps/web and libs/shared-ui, not the other 38 backend services.

Q: What is Git LFS (Large File Storage)?
An extension for versioning large binary files (videos, PSDs, datasets) efficiently, storing pointers in Git and actual files separately. Example: a game dev repo with large texture assets uses LFS so git clone doesn't download gigabytes of binary history every time.

Q: What's the difference between git merge --squash and a regular merge?

git merge --squash feature/login

Combines all commits from the feature branch into a single set of staged changes (you commit manually), with no merge commit and no individual commit history preserved. Example: a feature branch had 20 messy WIP commits — squash merging into main keeps main's history to one clean commit.

Q: Advanced scenario — how do you split one commit into multiple?

git rebase -i <commit>~1
# mark commit as 'edit'
git reset HEAD~1
git add <file1>
git commit -m "first part"
git add <file2>
git commit -m "second part"
git rebase --continue

Example: you committed both a bug fix and an unrelated refactor together — splitting keeps history clean and makes each commit revertable independently.

Q: Advanced scenario — how do you remove a sensitive file (like a leaked API key) from entire Git history?

git filter-repo --path secrets.env --invert-paths

(or the older git filter-branch / BFG Repo-Cleaner). Example: an .env file with AWS credentials was committed 6 months ago — deleting it now isn't enough since it's still in history; you must rewrite history and force-push, then rotate the leaked credentials immediately.

Q: What is git bisect run for automated bug hunting?

git bisect start
git bisect bad
git bisect good v1.0
git bisect run npm test
